# How long do animals sleep?
subtitle: "Guess how long a bat, a cat, a giraffe and a wild elephant sleep each day, and see why some sleep the day away while others barely sleep at all."

## How long do animals sleep?
text: {"font_size":34}
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Brown bat
      - Cat
      - Human
      - Giraffe
      - Elephant
    values:
      - 19.9
      - 12.5
      - 8
      - 4.6
      - 2
    icons:
      - bat
      - cat
      - person
      - giraffe
      - elephant
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 24
    y_label: Hours of sleep a day
sources:
    - id: siegel
      title: Clues to the functions of mammalian sleep
      authors: J. M. Siegel
      year: 2005
      doi: 10.1038/nature04285
      finding: bats sleep 18-20 hours a day, giraffes and elephants 3-4 (in captivity); sleep falls with body size in plant-eaters
    - id: gravett
      title: Inactivity/sleep in two wild free-roaming African elephant matriarchs
      authors: N. Gravett et al.
      year: 2017
      doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0171903
      finding: wild elephants slept about 2 hours a day, and sometimes went 46 hours without sleep

    card title "How long do animals sleep?"

How long does an animal sleep in a day? The answers range wider than you might think.
    draw axes

Start with us: an adult needs about eight hours.
    draw bar_3

    ask "How many hours do the others sleep? Drag each bar to your guess." on ["bar_1","bar_2","bar_4","bar_5"] store g default 12,14,6,4
        right: Close on all of them.
        wrong: Off by {g.off} hours on average.

The champion is the brown bat, asleep about twenty hours a day, and awake mainly at dusk, when the insects fly.
    highlight bar_1

A cat manages twelve hours or more: a hunter eats in a few big meals, and has few predators to fear.
    highlight bar_2

Giraffes in zoos sleep under five hours, in short naps, often standing up.
    highlight bar_4

And wild African elephants sleep about two hours a day, the least of any mammal measured.
    highlight bar_5

Researchers in Botswana put sleep trackers on two elephant matriarchs.
    highlight bar_5

Some nights, they went forty-six hours without any sleep at all.
    text awake "46 hours awake!" #b5482e cites ["gravett"] x 760 y 560 font_size 40

Big plant-eaters spend most of their day eating, and sleep little; a small animal in a safe roost can sleep the day away.
    highlight bar_4 bar_5
---
